`onPageUp`, `onPageDown`, `onHome`, and `onEnd` in `selectBoxCustom.ts` each had a while loop to skip disabled options, but if *all* remaining options were disabled the loop would exit still pointing to a disabled item — then call `setFocus`/`select` on it anyway.

## Changes

- **`onPageUp` / `onPageDown` / `onHome` / `onEnd`**: Use a local `candidate` variable for the skip loop; after the loop, check `this.options[candidate].isDisabled` and return early (without updating `this.selected`) if no enabled option was found.

```typescript
// Before
this.selected = 0;
while (this.selected < this.options.length - 1 && this.options[this.selected].isDisabled) {
    this.selected++;
}
this.selectList.setFocus([this.selected]); // could be a disabled item

// After
let candidate = 0;
while (candidate < this.options.length - 1 && this.options[candidate].isDisabled) {
    candidate++;
}
if (this.options[candidate].isDisabled) {
    return;
}
this.selected = candidate;
this.selectList.setFocus([this.selected]);
```

This aligns with the existing pattern in `onDownArrow`/`onUpArrow`, which already guard against going out of bounds before updating `this.selected`.

